<title>QATAR: Gulf Arab gas reserves hit 20.4 trln cubic metres.</title>
<headline>Gulf Arab gas reserves hit 20.4 trln cubic metres.</headline>
<dateline>DOHA 1997-02-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>Gas reserves in six oil-rich Gulf Arab states reached 20.4 trillion cubic metres in 1995 from 9.7 trillion cubic metres in 1986, the Gulf Cooperation Council chief said on Monday.</p>
<p>&quot;This represents an average growth of 6.8 percent a year,&quot; said Jameel al-Hujilan, secretary-general of the six-nation GCC, a political and economic alliance grouping Saudi Arabia, Kuwait, Oman, Bahrain, Qatar and the United Arab Emirates.</p>
<p>Crude oil reserves rose by 50 percent between 1986 and 1996, he told a ceremony in Qatar marking the opening of the Gulf Arab state's Ras Laffan industrial city and port. No outright figures were given for crude reserves.</p>
</text>
